  "id": "Compound::DB08882 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Linagliptin, a Dipeptidyl Peptidase 4 (DPP4) inhibitor, is clinically associated with the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through its mechanism of action as an inhibitor of DPP4, which is involved in the inactivation of incretin hormones. This relationship is supported by the shared classification of Linagliptin and Sitagliptin within the DPP4 inhibitor class, which in turn is linked to the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. The evidence suggests a direct therapeutic relationship between Linagliptin and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, mediated through its inhibitory effect on DPP4. The presence of a common ancestor in the DPP4 inhibitor class and the direct treatment link between Sitagliptin and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus reinforces this mechanistic connection. The evidence is consistent with a biologically plausible pathway, though the specific molecular mechanism by which Linagliptin exerts its therapeutic effect on diabetes remains to be fully elucidated.",
